.Sh DESCRIPTION
The simulator runs an application's procs on one thread, under an
interleaving and a virtual clock decided by a seed, and injects faults
drawn from the same seed.
The same seed and configuration replay byte for byte, so a failing seed
is a reproducer.
The header is
.Sy @stability experimental :
its surface may change in a minor release.
Every function links in every build.
Off a sim build they are dormant:
.Fn xtc_sim_exec_run
and
.Fn xtc_sim_fd_ready_at
return
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
the fault and buggify calls return 0, and the knobs have no effect.
The reverse holds on a sim build: its I/O backend has no kernel poller,
so outside a run
.Fn xtc_loop_run
.Pq Xr xtc_loop 3
and
.Fn xtc_exec_run
.Pq Xr xtc_exec 3
return
.Dv XTC_E_STATE
at once (and say why on stderr, once) instead of spinning on fds they
cannot see.
A program's real binary links a native libxtc; its simulation test
links the sim one.
.Ss Running
.Fn xtc_sim_exec_run
activates the simulator with
.Fa seed ,
enables the virtual clock at 0, and steps the loops of
.Fa e
until no proc can run and no timer or simulated event is pending, the
step budget
.Fa max_steps
runs out (0 or less: none), or
.Fn xtc_exec_stop
is called.
It deactivates the simulator before it returns.
Spawn the procs before the call.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_active
is 1 while a run (or
.Fn xtc_sim_activate )
is active.
.Fn xtc_sim_rand
draws a value in
.Bq 0, Fa bound
from the run's application stream, which no runtime decision draws from,
so a new draw in the application never moves the schedule; outside a
run it is
.Fn xtc_rand_u64
modulo
.Fa bound .
Draw every scripted choice of a test from it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_activate
and
.Fn xtc_sim_deactivate
start and stop the seeded streams outside
.Fn xtc_sim_exec_run ,
to draw from them in a unit test.
.Pp
The determinism guard traps a sim-reachable read of the real clock, the
environment or a raw thread id: in strict mode (the default) it aborts
and names the source;
.Fn xtc_sim_strict
with 0 makes it count instead, and
.Fn xtc_sim_nondeterminism_count
reads the count since the last activation.
.Fn xtc_sim_check
runs the structural invariants the scheduler checks after every step;
.Fn xtc_sim_state_hash
digests the loops' logical counters, for a replay comparison.
.Fn xtc_sim_set_consistency_check
installs a callback the run calls once at quiescence; a nonzero result
is the run's result.
It is cleared at the end of every run.
.Ss Simulated devices and threads
.Fn xtc_sim_fd_ready_at
makes
.Fa fd
ready for
.Fa revents
.Pq Dv XTC_IO_READABLE , XTC_IO_WRITABLE , XTC_IO_HUP , XTC_IO_ERR
from virtual time
.Fa due_ns
on.
A simulated device hands its client an fd it owns (a pipe, an eventfd)
as a completion fence and schedules it; a proc parked on it through
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
or
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
wakes at
.Fa due_ns .
The readiness is a level: every waiter whose interest overlaps
.Fa revents
wakes from
.Fa due_ns
on, including one that starts waiting later.
A second call for the same fd replaces the schedule;
.Fa revents
0 withdraws it.
No schedule survives the end of a run.
The fd is never read or written.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_external_begin
and
.Fn xtc_sim_external_complete
bring a completion that a plain OS thread produces, such as a device
worker that must not run on a fiber, under the seed.
.Fn xtc_sim_external_begin ,
called on a fiber of the run before the work is handed to the thread,
fixes the delivery's due time at now plus a seeded delay in
.Bq 0, Fa max_delay_ns ;
a
.Fa lane
from 0 to 15 delivers that lane in begin order, and -1 draws each delay
freely.
The thread calls
.Fn xtc_sim_external_complete
when its work is done.
At the due time the simulator waits, in real time, for that call if it
has not happened, then runs
.Fa fn Ns Pq Fa arg
on its own thread, outside any proc (a
.Fn xtc_send
from there is a send from a foreign thread).
The thread's real speed therefore never reaches the run.
Call
.Fn xtc_sim_external_complete
exactly once per token, also when the work failed.
.Pp
.Fa fn
runs on the only thread of the run, so nothing else runs while it
waits.
It uses
.Fn xtc_send ;
a full mailbox returns
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
and the message is still
.Fa fn Ns 's ,
to keep and retry, for example by handing it to a process that can
wait.
A wait for something only the run can do returns at once:
.Xr xtc_send_wait 3
on a full mailbox,
.Xr xtc_reg_whereis_wait 3
and
.Xr xtc_reg_send_wait 3
on a missing name, and an
.Xr xtc_svr_call 3
from outside a process return
.Er XTC_E_STATE .
A timed wait made on that thread outside a fiber
.Po Fn xtc_notify_wait , Fn xtc_future_wait , a lock with a timeout Pc
never sleeps, because virtual time cannot pass while the only thread is
blocked: it expires at once with
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
and reads no wall clock.
Outside a run
.Fn xtc_sim_external_begin
stores NULL in
.Fa *out ,
and
.Fn xtc_sim_external_complete
with a NULL token calls
.Fa fn Ns Pq Fa arg
at once, so production code calls both unconditionally.
.Ss Pessimal paths and faults
.Fn XTC_SIM_BUGGIFY
.Pq Fn xtc_sim_buggify
marks a decision point where the code may take a legal but pessimal
path: the smallest batch, a spurious retry, the slow branch.
The first time a run reaches a name, a coin with the
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_enable
probability decides it, and every later reach of that name in the run
gets the same answer.
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_fault
is a fresh per-call coin from the same stream, for
.Ql if (XTC_SIM_BUGGIFY(name) && xtc_sim_buggify_fault(250)) .
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_reached_count ,
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_active_count
and
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_site
report which sites a run reached and which were active, so a sweep can
measure the fault space it covered.
.Fn xtc_sim_fault
is a per-call coin on the fault stream.
.Fn XTC_SIM_FAULT_POINT
.Pq Fn xtc_sim_fault_point
marks a critical section;
.Fn xtc_sim_fault_points_enable ,
.Fn xtc_sim_fault_point_fires
and
.Fn xtc_sim_fault_points_seen
arm and count them.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_io_faults_enable
defers file-AIO completions by a seeded latency and fails a fraction of
them (a short transfer, or EIO on a sync).
.Fn xtc_sim_io_corrupt_enable
tears writes (a prefix persists, full success is reported) and flips a
byte of reads.
.Fn xtc_sim_io_enospc_enable
fails writes with ENOSPC.
.Fn xtc_sim_io_stale_enable
returns an overwritten version on reads.
.Fn xtc_sim_io_wb_enable
models a write-back cache: after a simulated crash,
.Fn xtc_sim_io_durable_end
is the last byte of
.Fa fd
that an fsync confirmed.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_partition_set ,
.Fn xtc_sim_partition_isolate
and
.Fn xtc_sim_partition_clear
drop messages between loops (identified by executor slot + 1).
A monitor's DOWN and a link's EXIT still cross a cut, as they would in a
real process;
.Fn xtc_sim_net_latency
delays each cross-loop message by a seeded latency.
.Fn xtc_sim_sched_pessimal
starves loops on purpose;
.Fn xtc_sim_swizzle_enable
reorders deliveries one slot.
.Ss Planted bugs
.Fn XTC_DST_BUG n
is 1 only in a build made with
.Fl DXTC_DST_INJECT_BUG Ns = Ns Ar n ,
and 0 otherwise.
Guard a deliberate defect with it and prove that a sweep of a few seeds
catches it: that bug-detection latency, not code coverage, is the
measure of a simulation test.
.Ss Clock
.Fn xtc_sim_exec_run
drives the virtual clock itself.
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_enable ,
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_disable ,
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_advance
and
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_set
drive it by hand outside a run;
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_active
says whether it is on.
Under the virtual clock,
.Fn xtc_clock_mono
returns virtual time.
Code that derives a wall-clock-like value must then add a fixed
constant to it, never an offset sampled from real time: such an offset
carries real time into the run, and the guard cannot see it.
.Fn xtc_sim_clock_skew
offsets (and jitters) what fibers read without changing when timers
fire.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_sim_exec_run
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
at quiescence or on
.Fn xtc_exec_stop ,
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
when the budget ran out with work left,
.Dv XTC_E_DEADLK
when procs are alive but nothing can wake them,
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
on a broken structural invariant or a counted determinism violation,
the consistency check's nonzero result,
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
off a sim build, or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL executor.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sim_fd_ready_at
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
(a negative fd or time, a bit outside the four above),
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
(64 fds already scheduled), or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Fn xtc_sim_external_begin
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
(NULL
.Fa out ,
a negative delay, a lane past 15),
.Dv XTC_E_STATE
(under a run but not on one of its fibers), or
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM .
.Fn xtc_sim_external_complete
returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
.Fn xtc_sim_buggify_site
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
past the last site.
The predicates and counters return the value described above.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A test of an application's own proc: one seed sweep, each seed run
twice to prove the replay.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int total;

static void
worker(void *arg)
{
	int i, n = 1 + (int)xtc_sim_rand(8);
	(void)arg;
	for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
		if (XTC_SIM_BUGGIFY("worker.slow"))
			xtc_proc_sleep(1000000);
		total++;
	}
}

static int
run(uint64_t seed, int *out)
{
	xtc_exec_t *e;
	int i, rc;
	total = 0;
	xtc_exec_create(&(xtc_exec_opts_t){
	    .size = sizeof(xtc_exec_opts_t), .n_loops = 2 }, &e);
	for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
		xtc_proc_spawn(xtc_exec_loop(e, i % 2), worker, NULL,
		    NULL, NULL);
	xtc_sim_buggify_enable(500);
	rc = xtc_sim_exec_run(e, seed, 100000);
	xtc_sim_buggify_disable();
	xtc_exec_destroy(e);
	*out = total;
	return rc;
}
.Ed
.Pp
The complete program is
.Pa docs/_includes/snippets/20_dst_app.c ;
it compiles in every build and runs only against a sim build.
